>
  <p>Finals are less about how many hours you put in and more about where you put them. Most students spread effort evenly or chase whatever feels urgent, and run out of time on the exam that mattered most. A short planning session fixes that.</p>

  <h2 id="triage">Rank your exams first</h2>
  <p>Before you open a book, list every exam with three numbers: how much it is worth, how hard it is for you, and how far behind you are. Multiply gut-feel scores together and you get a priority order. A heavily weighted subject you are weak in beats an easy one you have already half-mastered, even if the easy one is sooner. Use the <a href="/tools/gpa-calculator/">GPA calculator</a> to see which results actually move your grade.</p>

  <h2 id="plan">A two-week plan</h2>
  <p>Put every exam date into the <a href="/tools/exam-countdown/">exam countdown</a> so you can see the days and study days left. Then split the two weeks into two passes: the first week to relearn and make recall questions, the second to test yourself and sit past papers under time. The <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan generator</a> turns your subjects and dates into dated blocks with revision built in. Leave two or three empty catch-up slots a week, because something always runs over.</p>

  <h2 id="method">What to actually do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Past papers under exam conditions.</strong> Nothing predicts exam performance like practising the exam.</li>
    <li><strong>Self-test</strong> from memory, then check. Do not re-read notes and call it revision.</li>
    <li><strong>Interleave</strong> two or three subjects a day rather than one marathon.</li>
    <li><strong>Review mistakes</strong>, not just scores. Your errors are your real syllabus.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="final">The last 48 hours</h2>
  <p>Switch from learning new material to light review and confidence: skim your summary sheets, run through flagged questions, and stop early enough to sleep. Diekelmann and Born's work on sleep and memory is clear that the night before matters. Walk into the exam rested, not wrung out. For the tighter version of this plan, see <a href="/guides/how-to-study-for-an-exam-in-one-week/">how to study for an exam in one week</a> and <a href="/guides/how-to-study-for-multiple-exams/">how to study for multiple exams</a>.</p>
</Guide>
